.TH CCARCHIVE 1 "2026-10-05" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccarchive \- durably preserve every Claude Code transcript, word for word
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccarchive
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-verify
.RI [ \-\-materialise ]
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-audit
.RI [ \-\-materialise ]
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-restore
.RI [ \-\-delta ]
.RI [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RI [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-rekey
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.br
.B ccarchive
.BR \-\-lock\-status " | " \-\-clear\-lock
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-install\-schedule
.RI [ \-\-at
.IR HH:MM ]
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.br
.B ccarchive
.BR \-\-schedule\-status " | " \-\-uninstall\-schedule
.SH DESCRIPTION
Claude Code records each session as a raw JSON-lines log under
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ <encoded-repo>/ <session-uuid> .jsonl
(plus subagent logs beneath it). That file is the complete, word-for-word
record of the session \(em every message, timestamp, model, token count, tool
call and thinking block. It is also
.BR ephemeral :
Claude Code deletes logs older than its
.I cleanupPeriodDays
setting (30 by default), so the history disappears on a rolling window.
.PP
.B ccarchive
mirrors every one of those logs \(em and the offloaded payloads, subagent
metadata, memory and history that go with them (see
.BR CAPTURE ) \(em
into a compressed, append-only archive that outlives that cleanup. Where
.BR cctranscript (1)
.I reads
the logs,
.B ccarchive
.I keeps
them.
.PP
The archive is an
.B incremental per-file gzip mirror :
each captured file
.IR <rel>/<name>
under the source becomes
.IR <dest>/<rel>/<name> .gz
under the archive. Only files that changed since the last run are
recompressed, so it is cheap to run often and light on a synced destination
such as iCloud Drive (only new or updated files upload). Each archived
session stays individually readable \(em decompress it and hand it to
.BR cctranscript (1),
or search the whole archive with
.BR zgrep (1).
.PP
The tool is
.B append-only by contract:
it never deletes from the archive, and it never overwrites a transcript's
archived copy with anything but that transcript with more added at the end.
When Claude Code's cleanup removes a source log, the archived copy remains
\(em that is the whole point. It does not parse the JSON, it preserves the
bytes, so it is immune to any change in Claude Code's internal log format.
.SH DESIGN
What the tool is for, as plain goals. Where a goal is not built yet, it says so.
.IP \(bu 2
Signed for integrity: the index of what was archived carries a signature,
checked
before anything is written (see
.BR SIGNING
and
.BR TRUST ).
.IP \(bu 2
Encrypted for confidentiality: planned, \fBnot yet built\fR. Today the
archive is
readable by anyone who can read the destination.
.IP \(bu 2
Compressed: each file is gzipped, so a session stays individually readable.
.IP \(bu 2
Writes limited to what changed: only new or changed files are written, which
also
keeps a synced destination light.
.IP \(bu 2
Handles storage that offloads data and leaves a stub (iCloud): an evicted
file is
named and skipped, never read in a way that forces a download or aborts the run
(see
.BR DATALESS ).
.IP \(bu 2
A manifest, so the archive is cheap to manage and consume: what is held, with
hashes, can be read without opening every file.
.IP \(bu 2
Efficient to use from the sibling tools:
.BR ccrepo (1)
and
.BR cctranscript (1)
read the archive directly (\fB\-\-from\-archive\fR).
.SH CAPTURE
What
.B ccarchive
captures is an
.B allowlist,
not "everything under the source root": each class below is preserved as raw
bytes; anything unrecognised is left out by default. This is stated here because
a silent exclusion is invisible \(em an archive that dropped referenced content
while advertising completeness would be dishonest about what it holds.
.PP
.B Captured.
.TP
.I <encoded-repo>/<uuid>.jsonl
Session transcripts, and nested subagent logs beneath the session
.RI ( <uuid>/subagents/*.jsonl ) \(em
the core record, at any depth.
.TP
.I <uuid>/subagents/*.meta.json
.B Subagent metadata sidecars.
Claude Code writes one small JSON file beside each subagent log recording how
that agent was spawned: its type and description, the
.I tool_use
id of the block that spawned it, its spawn depth and parent agent, the model it
ran on, and any worktree it was given. None of that is recoverable from the
transcripts alone \(em the
.I tool_use
id is the only join back to the spawning block in the parent session log, and
the depth and parent are what disambiguate an agent that itself spawned agents
into the same directory. Matched by name
.RI ( *.meta.json )
but
.B scoped to a subagents/ directory,
so a
.I .meta.json
appearing anywhere else stays excluded by default like any other unrecognised
file, rather than being swept in sight-unseen.
.TP
.IR <uuid>/tool-results/* " and " <uuid>/toolu_*
.B Tool-result sidecars.
When a tool output is large, Claude Code offloads the payload to one of these
files and leaves only a pointer in the
.I .jsonl.
Capturing them closes a real hole: without them an archived transcript can
dangle a reference to content that was pruned from the live store and never
preserved. The modern shape is
.IR tool-results/ ;
.I toolu_*
is the legacy sibling. Both are opaque bytes \(em taken as-is, whatever the
extension.
.TP
.I <project>/memory/*.md
.B Per-project auto-memory
\(em the durable, cross-session working state Claude Code keeps for a project
(a memory index and its named entries). It is not a transcript, but it is part
of the working record and lives nowhere else.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/history.jsonl
.B The top-level prompt/command history.
This one file sits
.I beside
the projects directory, one level
.I above
the source root, so capturing it is a deliberate reach up out of
.IR ~/.claude/projects .
It is archived under a reserved
.I _external/
path that cannot collide with an encoded project directory, and
.B \-\-restore
returns it to its real home above the source root \(em never rewritten inside
.IR projects/ .
Override its location with
.B CCARCHIVE_HISTORY
(mainly for testing).
.PP
.B Two kinds of data.
Every captured file is one of two kinds, and the kind decides what a change to
it means. The mapping lives in one place in the code and is stated here:
.TP
.B append-only records
Session transcripts and the subagent logs beneath them (every
.I .jsonl
at any depth), the external prompt history, and tool-result sidecars. Once
written, the only legitimate change is growth at the end, however long after the
last write (a paused session picked up again). Any other change \(em a shrink,
or
a rewrite in place \(em is an
.BR anomaly :
the archived copy is never overwritten and the run exits 1. See
.BR "KEPT COPIES" .
.TP
.B living documents
Per-project memory notes
.RI ( memory/*.md )
and subagent metadata sidecars
.RI ( *.meta.json ),
which Claude Code (or other software) alters over time. A change that is not a
pure addition is expected: the previous mirror is kept as a dated version, the
new one becomes current, and the run exits 0. See
.BR "KEPT COPIES" .
.PP
.B Counts.
The summary and
.B \-\-json
keep the file total and add what a person recognises:
.I sessions
(only a top-level
.IR <encoded-repo>/<uuid> .jsonl
counts as a session),
.I subagentTranscripts
(any
.I .jsonl
nested below a session), and a per-kind breakdown
.RI ( kinds :
session, subagentTranscript, history, toolResult, subagentMeta, memory \(em each
with
.I files
in the live store and
.I archived
by this run). The one session count answers "how many sessions"; the file total
does not, because one session can start dozens of subagents. A second block,
.IR inArchive ,
counts everything the signed index holds, sources since pruned included. The
field set is the same on every run, with known zeros printed.
.PP
.B Not captured.
The following are deliberately excluded and, on a machine wipe, are not
recoverable from the archive:
.IR <project>/webfetch-*.pdf
(WebFetch page snapshots \(em re-fetchable, marginal),
.I ~/.claude/file-history/
(per-session edit checkpoints \(em large, and overlapping repository git
history),
.IR ~/.claude/sessions/ ", " ~/.claude/session-env/ " and " ~/.claude/shell-snapshots/
(ephemeral per-session runtime and shell state), and Claude Code's
.IR config ", " plugins " and " caches
.RI ( settings*.json ", " ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md ", " plugins/ ", " backups/ ", the various cache directories)."
These are runtime, re-derivable, or config that belongs to its own backup story,
not to a transcript archive.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dest " dir"
Archive into
.I dir
instead of the default, which is a
.I cc-transcripts
folder in the operator's iCloud Drive. May also be set with the
.B CCARCHIVE_DEST
environment variable.
.TP
.BI \-\-source " dir"
Read transcripts from
.I dir
instead of
.IR ~/.claude/projects .
Mainly for testing.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be archived (or, with
.BR \-\-restore ,
restored) and write nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Resolve an anomaly deliberately: make a live file that is
.I not
an append to its archived copy the archive's current copy. The previous mirror
is kept as a dated version (see
.BR "KEPT COPIES" ),
never discarded, and the changed live version already kept aside stays where it
is. It changes nothing else: an ordinary append, a living document's edit and a
first capture are written the same way with or without it. With
.BR \-\-restore ,
it also overrides the refusal to overwrite a live file that is newer than the
archived copy, and the refusal to restore an entry that has no recorded sha256
\(em see
.B RESTORE
below. It never overrides a restore whose archived bytes fail their recorded
sha256.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-repo\-dest
Permit an archive directory that sits inside a git work tree. Refused by
default: the archive holds personal transcripts, and a repository destination
is one commit away from publishing them.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Re-hash every archived transcript and compare it against the sha256 recorded
when it was archived, and check the manifest's own signature. A mirror that will
not decompress is reported by name; it does not stop the check. See
.B INTEGRITY
and
.B SIGNING
below.
.TP
.B \-\-rekey
Mint a fresh signing key (replacing any existing one) and re-sign the current
manifest with it. The deliberate key roll \(em after suspected key exposure, or
on a cadence \(em and the one deliberate way to clear an index a run refused to
trust: run it only once you are satisfied the archive is intact. Takes the lock.
See
.B SIGNING
and
.B TRUST
below.
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Compare the
.I live
store against the archive and report how it has drifted from what was
preserved \(em mutated, renamed, grown, new or pruned transcripts. Read-only.
See
.B AUDIT
below.
.TP
.B \-\-materialise
With
.B \-\-verify
or
.BR \-\-audit ,
read files whose local bytes iCloud has evicted (see
.B DATALESS
below) instead of skipping them. This deliberately
.I re-downloads
each evicted file to hash it, so it is opt-in \(em the default skips them to
keep a routine check from dragging the whole archive back onto disk. No effect
on any other mode.
.TP
.B \-\-restore
Restore transcripts from the archive back into the live store: gunzip each
.IR <dest>/<rel> .jsonl.gz
to
.IR <source>/<rel> .jsonl.
By default every archived transcript is a target (rebuild a lost store); with
.B \-\-delta
only the drift
.BR \-\-audit
reports is. Writes only under the source. See
.B RESTORE
below.
.TP
.B \-\-delta
With
.BR \-\-restore ,
restore only what
.B \-\-audit
flags as drift \(em the
.BR mutated ,
.B pruned
and
.B renamed
buckets \(em rather than the whole archive. Ignored without
.BR \-\-restore .
.TP
.B \-\-lock\-status
Say whether a run holds this archive's lock:
.BR free ,
.B held
by a live run (with its pid, host, start time and last heartbeat), or
.B STUCK
\(em the holder is not running. Read-only. See
.B LOCK
below.
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-lock
Clear a stuck lock. Refuses, naming the holder, while the holder is still
running, and refuses a lock taken on another host (its liveness cannot be
checked from here). See
.B LOCK
below.
.TP
.BI "\-\-install\-schedule [\-\-at " HH:MM ]
Set up a daily run at a clock time, local, 24-hour; the default is
.BR 03:00 .
This is a real calendar schedule, not a run at login: launchd on macOS, a
systemd
user timer on Linux. Re-running it replaces the old schedule cleanly. A
.B \-\-dest
given here is baked into the scheduled command; without one the scheduled run
uses the default destination. The installer never runs an archive itself. Other
platforms are refused (exit status 2). See
.BR SCHEDULING .
.TP
.BI \-\-at " HH:MM"
The time of day for
.BR \-\-install\-schedule .
Two digits each, 24-hour (\c
.BR 03:00 ,
.BR 16:45 ).
Anything else is refused before anything is written.
.TP
.B \-\-schedule\-status
Read-only. Says whether a schedule file exists, the time it is set for, and
whether the scheduler has it loaded (macOS) or enabled and active (Linux). It
also names an older login-style agent if one is found.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall\-schedule
Unload the schedule, and delete its files. An older login-style agent (one that
ran at every login) is removed the same way, and the report says so. Nothing in
the archive is touched.
.PP
The three schedule options are each a mode of their own: they cannot be combined
with one another or with an archive, verify, audit, restore or lock option
(\c
.B \-\-dest
and
.B \-\-at
go with
.B \-\-install\-schedule
only), so a scheduling command can never also change an archive.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a machine-readable JSON summary instead of the human report. Never
styled; safe to pipe into
.BR jq (1).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a concise usage summary and exit. This manual is the full reference.
.PP
Any other option \(em a typo such as
.BR \-\-dry\-runn ,
a stray word, or
.B \-\-dest
with no directory after it \(em is refused by name with exit status 2 before
anything is read or written, so a mistyped preview can never run a real archive.
.SH RUNNING IT
.B ccarchive
can be run by hand, or on a schedule you ask for with
.BR \-\-install\-schedule .
It installs nothing unless you ask. An archive is only as current as its last
run,
so run it more often than Claude Code's
.I cleanupPeriodDays
(30 by default): a session whose source is pruned before the first run that sees
it is not captured. Running it often is cheap, because only new or changed files
are written.
.SH SCHEDULING
.B \-\-install\-schedule
sets up one run a day at a clock time (default 03:00, local; change it with
.BR \-\-at ).
It is a calendar schedule: it never runs at login, and installing it does not
run an archive. It does not touch the archive.
.SS macOS
A user LaunchAgent, label
.BR com.ccarchive.archive ,
in
.IR ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ ,
with a
.B StartCalendarInterval
of the chosen hour and minute. It has no
.BR RunAtLoad ,
no
.B StartInterval
and no
.BR KeepAlive .
The agent runs the absolute path of the
.B node
that installed it and the absolute path of the script, adding
.B \-\-dest
only if you gave one. Its output goes to
.IR ~/Library/Logs/ccarchive.log .
It is loaded with
.BR "launchctl bootstrap gui/<uid>" .
If you later change which
.B node
you use (a version manager, an upgrade) or move the script, run
.B \-\-install\-schedule
again so the agent points at the new paths.
.PP
If the Mac is asleep at the scheduled time, launchd runs the missed slot once
when it wakes (cron does not, which is why cron is not used). macOS lists every
background job, scheduled or not, under System Settings, General, Login Items,
.BR "Allow in the Background" :
that is an allowlist of what may run in the background, not a list of what opens
at login, and the entry is named after the program the agent starts.
.SS Linux
A systemd
.B user
timer and service under
.IR ~/.config/systemd/user/ :
.I ccarchive.service
(Type=oneshot, running the same absolute command) and
.I ccarchive.timer
with
.BR "OnCalendar=*-*-* HH:MM:00" .
.B Persistent=true
is set, so a run missed because the machine was off or asleep is caught up the
next time the timer is active. It is enabled with
.B systemctl \-\-user daemon-reload
and then
.BR "systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now ccarchive.timer" ;
output goes to the journal
.RB ( "journalctl \-\-user \-u ccarchive.service" ).
.PP
A user timer only runs while the user has a session (logged in, or a user
manager
kept alive) unless lingering is enabled with
.BR "loginctl enable-linger" .
ccarchive does
.B not
enable lingering; that is a machine-wide choice for you to make.
.PP
If
.B systemctl \-\-user
cannot be reached, the installer refuses (exit status 2), installs nothing, and
prints a crontab line to paste yourself. Cron does not catch up a missed run.
.SS Other platforms
Refused with exit status 2; nothing is written.
.SS Environment
A scheduled run does not see your shell's environment, so
.B CCARCHIVE_DEST
and
.B CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE
set in a shell do not reach it. Give
.B \-\-dest
at install time if you archive somewhere other than the default.
.SS Checking and removing
.B \-\-schedule\-status
shows what is installed. To remove it:
.BR "ccarchive \-\-uninstall\-schedule" .
That unloads the agent (macOS) or disables the timer (Linux) and deletes the
files. It also removes an older login-style agent, one with
.B RunAtLoad
or
.BR StartInterval ,
which an earlier version installed under the same label. Done by hand on macOS:
.B launchctl bootout gui/$(id \-u)/com.ccarchive.archive
and delete the plist.
.SH INTEGRITY
gzip carries a CRC-32, so a corrupted
.I .gz
fails to decompress \(em but that is weak and only proves the file is
self-consistent, not that it still equals what was captured. So when it
archives a transcript,
.B ccarchive
records a sha256 of the transcript's raw bytes in
.IR <dest>/manifest.json .
.PP
.B ccarchive \-\-verify
re-hashes every archived
.I .gz
and compares it against the manifest, reporting any
.B mismatch
(mutation, or a bit flip that still decompresses),
.B missing
file, or
.B corrupt
file (one that will not decompress at all: bit rot, a truncated copy, a file
that
is not gzip), and exiting non-zero if the archive does not verify. A corrupt or
otherwise unreadable mirror is named and the check carries on with the next
file;
it never ends the verify. The manifest tracks
the
.I archive
(append-only), not the live sources: a session pruned by Claude Code's cleanup
keeps its recorded hash because its
.I .gz
is kept.
.PP
A file present in the archive but absent from the manifest is reported
.B UNMANIFESTED
and fails the verify: it is either injected, or history the manifest lost, and
both deserve a human eye. The next ordinary run adopts it only if it is
byte-identical to the live copy, and otherwise moves it aside (see
.BR TRUST ).
Entries an earlier version wrote for files whose source was pruned before
hashing existed are marked
.B fromArchive
\(em their hash attests the archived copy only, not the original raw bytes \(em
and
.B \-\-verify
counts them distinctly. Runs no longer create such entries.
.PP
A
.B MISMATCH
is reported with its next step: the archived bytes are not the ones the signed
index recorded (bit rot, a sync glitch or tampering), and the copy is left
untouched;
.B \-\-audit
shows whether the live copy still matches the record. Keep the copy until you
have decided. A
.B MISSING
copy may have been deleted, or set aside by a run that found it did not match
its record. Files under
.I _untrusted/
are counted, not checked.
.PP
The archive never
.I deletes
\(em but an ordinary run does
.I replace
an archived copy when the source is newer, so what counts as a legitimate
change matters (see
.B CAPTURE,
two kinds of data). A change is tested without reading the mirror: the live file
is a
.B pure append
exactly when it is at least as long as the length recorded at capture
.RI ( rawBytes )
and the sha256 of its first
.I rawBytes
bytes equals the signed entry's sha256. A pure append \(em including no change
at
all, and growth long after the last write \(em is written normally. Anything
else
depends on the kind:
.IP \(bu 2
an
.B append-only record
that shrank, or was rewritten in place (same size or not), is an
.BR anomaly :
the archived mirror is
.I never
overwritten, the changed live version is kept aside (see
.BR "KEPT COPIES" ),
the report says so plainly, and the run exits 1.
.B \-\-force
is the deliberate resolution. This replaced an older rule under which a
same-size rewrite quietly replaced the mirror and only a smaller source was
refused.
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B living document
that changed other than by appending keeps its previous mirror as a dated
version, then the new one is written; the run exits 0 and counts the versions
kept.
.PP
The mirror image \(em a source restored from backup with an
.I older
modification time \(em is silently left un-archived, since the archived copy is
newer;
.B \-\-audit
shows it.
.PP
A sibling check is the
.B layout-drift alarm :
a run that finds
.I zero
transcripts under the source while the manifest already records some exits
non-zero instead of reporting success \(em the live directory moved or Claude
Code changed where it writes logs, and a silently empty mirror would stop
growing while looking healthy.
.PP
The sha256 manifest defends against
.I accidental
corruption. On its own it is not tamper-proof \(em an adversary who rewrote a
.I .gz
.I and
the manifest would pass. That gap is closed by
.BR SIGNING ,
below: the manifest is signed with a key kept off the archive, so a forged
manifest fails
.BR \-\-verify ,
and a run that would write refuses it rather than re-sign it (see
.BR TRUST ).
.SH KEPT COPIES
Two reserved areas under the archive root hold copies a run keeps rather than
overwrite or discard. Both are recorded in the signed manifest, so they are
covered by its signature and checked by
.B \-\-verify
(a damaged one fails the verify, like any archived file). Neither is a mirror:
every walk,
.B \-\-restore
and
.B \-\-audit
leaves them out, and nothing ever deletes or overwrites one. Both live at the
archive root, like
.I _untrusted/
and
.IR _external/ ,
rather than beside each mirror, so they cannot collide with an encoded project
directory and the other instruments skip them in one place.
.TP
.I <dest>/_anomalies/<UTC stamp>/<rel>.gz
The changed
.I live
version of an append-only record that shrank or was rewritten in place. The
archived mirror stays exactly as it was. Each distinct live version is kept
once: the run reports it as an
.BR ANOMALY ,
and every later run in which the live file still hashes the same reports one
line,
.BR "STILL DIFFERS" ,
naming the copy and the date it was kept, without copying it again. Exit 1.
Look at it, then either leave it (the archive keeps the original) or run
.B ccarchive \-\-force
to make the live version current.
.TP
.I <dest>/_versions/<UTC stamp>/<rel>.gz
The previous mirror of a living document (or of an anomaly resolved with
.BR \-\-force ),
kept before the new one is written. The old mirror is
.I renamed
into place, never read, so an iCloud-offloaded mirror is kept as readily as a
local one. The rename is journalled first, so a run killed before the index is
saved is repaired from its intent journal (see
.BR TRUST ).
Expected behaviour, exit 0; the report counts versions kept
.RI ( versionsKept
under
.BR \-\-json ).
.PP
The stamp is the run's start time in UTC. Two keeps of one file in the same
second
get a numbered stamp (\fI\-2\fR, \fI\-3\fR), never an overwrite.
.PP
.B Getting an older version.
.B \-\-restore
restores the current version only. To get an earlier state of a living document,
decompress the kept copy yourself:
.B gunzip \-c <dest>/_versions/<stamp>/<rel>.gz > <file>
(list them with
.BR "find <dest>/_versions \-name '*.gz'" ;
the oldest stamp is the oldest state kept).
.SH SIGNING
The manifest is the trust anchor, but a manifest an attacker can rewrite anchors
nothing: with write access to the archive volume (for the default iCloud Drive
destination, anyone or anything that can write there) a tamperer could rewrite a
.I .gz
.I and
recompute the manifest hash, and a hash-only
.B \-\-verify
would pass. So
.B ccarchive
.B signs
the manifest.
.PP
When it writes the manifest, it computes an
.B HMAC\-SHA256
over the manifest's exact bytes using a secret key held
.I off
the archive volume, and writes a detached signature beside it as
.IR manifest.json.sig
(algorithm, a non-secret key fingerprint, and the MAC).
.B \-\-verify
recomputes the MAC with the local key: a manifest altered after signing no
longer carries a MAC the key reproduces, so the tampering is
.BR "tamper-evident" .
Every manifest write re-signs \(em but only after the run has authenticated the
manifest it loaded (see
.BR TRUST ),
so a run never launders a forged index into a signed one. A pre-signing
(unsigned) archive is therefore not migrated silently: a run refuses it, and the
operator migrates it deliberately with
.BR \-\-rekey .
.PP
The manifest is saved, and re-signed, every 50 mirrors written (the checkpoint
interval), when the run finishes, and again if the run fails part way with an
error it can catch. What a failure leaves behind depends on how it ends. A
.B thrown
error (a full disk, say) is caught, and the manifest saved
before the error leaves it covering every mirror the run had written. A
.B hard kill
(power loss, SIGKILL) cannot run any code: it can leave up to 50 mirrors ahead
of the saved manifest, which
.B \-\-verify
then reports as
.BR UNMANIFESTED .
The intent journal described under
.B TRUST
is what puts exactly those back at the next run, and only those. A manifest
entry
is only ever set after its mirror is fully written. Overlapping runs are
prevented by a lock (see
.BR LOCK ).
.PP
The manifest and its signature are two files, and a write cannot replace both at
once. So the new signature is written first, beside the old one, as
.IR manifest.json.sig.next ;
then the manifest is replaced; then the
.I .next
file is renamed over the signature. A kill at any point leaves a pair that
verifies: before the manifest rename, the old pair; after it, the new manifest
with its signature in
.IR .next ,
which
.B \-\-verify
and a run accept (and say so) only if it verifies under the key over the
manifest
bytes actually on disk, and the next run finishes the swap; after the last
rename, the new pair. During an ordinary save there is no window in which a kill
makes the manifest read as tampered.
.PP
The scheme is symmetric (HMAC), not a public-key signature, by design: the
signer and the verifier are the same machine and user, so a locally-held key is
the simplest fit. A keypair would buy something only if the archive had to be
verified somewhere the signing key must not go \(em not the case here, and a
public key
shipped
.I inside
the archive would just be replaced by the same write-capable tamperer.
.PP
.B The key.
The key is stored, hex-encoded and mode
.BR 0600 ,
at
.I ~/.claude/ccarchive-signing.key
by default \(em on local disk, beside Claude Code's own data, never synced into
the archive. Set
.B CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE
to override the path; a non-interactive run (from cron, say) reads it there
without a Keychain prompt (a file is chosen over the macOS Keychain precisely
because background contexts cannot reliably reach the Keychain, and this key
guards tamper-evidence, not a confidential value). It is
minted at first use with 32 bytes of entropy \(em never a hand-composed value.
.PP
.B Rotation.
The key is re-mintable at will:
.B \-\-rekey
generates a fresh one and re-signs the current manifest, which is all a roll
needs \(em the signature only ever attests the
.I current
manifest, so nothing is lost. Roll on any credible suspicion of key exposure,
and on a cadence besides. But because the key is what a
.I new
machine needs to verify existing signatures, keep an out-of-band backup of it: a
lost key cannot be re-minted to a value that verifies old signatures (the same
redundancy obligation atelier's secrets doctrine places on a store's master
key).
.PP
.B Failure semantics.
.B \-\-verify
never reports an unverified manifest as clean. It distinguishes, and exits
non-zero for, each non-green case: a
.B MISMATCH
(the manifest changed after signing \(em tamper evident); a manifest signed by a
.B different key
(an unexpected roll, or a foreign signer); an
.B unsigned
manifest (a pre-signing archive to migrate, or a removed signature); and an
.B unverifiable
manifest \(em no key on this machine, so the signature cannot be checked at all
(bring the key out-of-band; a run will not mint a new one while a manifest
exists). Only a manifest whose
signature the local key reproduces exits
.BR 0 .
The signature is
.I tamper-evident,
not tamper-proof: it detects a forgery at verify time, it does not prevent the
write, and an attacker who also steals the key can forge a valid signature \(em
which is why the key's confidentiality and cheap rotation matter.
.SH TRUST
The signed manifest is the index of what
.B ccarchive
itself wrote. It is also cheaper to read than the archive, so a run decides what
it trusts from the index and a
.BR stat (2)
of each mirror, and reads a mirror's bytes only when the two disagree.
.PP
.B Authenticate before any write.
An ordinary run and
.B \-\-restore
check the manifest's signature before they use it, over the exact bytes they
then parse, and refuse anything but
.B verified
\(em a manifest changed after signing, one signed by a different key, an
unsigned one, one that cannot be checked because there is no key here, or a
signature whose manifest has gone. The refusal names the state, changes nothing
and exits 2. A missing key is never minted while a manifest exists: that would
re-sign an index nobody checked. A genuinely new archive \(em no manifest
and no signature \(em proceeds,
and is signed as it is written. The one deliberate clear
is
.BR \-\-rekey ,
once the operator is satisfied the archive is intact. A dry run refuses the same
way, since what it would preview is a refusal.
.PP
.B The intent journal.
Before a run writes each mirror it appends one record to a private journal
beside the signing key, off the archive (see
.BR FILES ):
the mirror's path, the sha256 and length of the exact bytes it is about to
write, the run's id and the time. The same record is written before a kept copy
is
renamed or written under
.I _versions/
or
.I _anomalies/
(see
.BR "KEPT COPIES" ). Each record is flushed to disk and signed with
the signing key, so an edited record is detected. At each checkpoint, and when
it finishes, the run cuts the journal down to what is still pending and removes
it when nothing is. A journal found when a run starts therefore proves the
previous run died part way. For each record, if the mirror is there and its
decompressed bytes hash to what the record says, the index entry is set from the
record (a kept copy is recorded the same way) and the run reports it
.BR REPAIRED .
An evicted mirror is not read: its record stays pending and it is reported
.BR "not checked (offloaded)" .
A mirror that does not match its record is not adopted from the journal; the
rule below then applies to it. A record whose signature fails is never used,
and the run exits 1.
.PP
.B Anything else the index does not vouch for.
Each index entry a run writes records the mirror's own size and modification
time as written. A mirror is vouched for when its
.BR stat (2)
agrees with its signed entry \(em no read needed. A mirror with no entry, or
whose size or time disagrees with its entry, and that the journal does not
explain, is handled in this order:
.IP \(bu 2
its bytes still hash to the signed entry \(em only its size or time changed (a
sync tool re-stamped it, say); the entry's size and time are refreshed;
.IP \(bu 2
its bytes are identical (sha256) to the live copy \(em it is
.B ADOPTED
into the index;
.IP \(bu 2
anything else \(em it is
.B moved
to
.IR <dest>/_untrusted/<UTC\ stamp>/<rel>.gz :
never deleted, never overwritten (a second one under the same name gets a
numbered suffix), never adopted. The live copy, if there is one, is then
archived normally, and the run exits 1 explaining what it set aside and why.
.PP
Files under
.I _untrusted/
are not part of the archive: every walk,
.B \-\-verify
and
.B \-\-restore
leave them out
.RB ( \-\-verify
counts them). Look at them, then keep or remove them yourself. An evicted mirror
is never read to make this decision: it is reported
.B "not checked (offloaded)"
and left where it is, and if its live copy has changed, that change is reported
.B NOT ARCHIVED
(exit 1) rather than written over a copy nobody checked.
.PP
An entry written by a version without the size and time fields (a
.B legacy
entry; most closed transcripts, which never change again, would otherwise only
ever be checked by
.BR \-\-verify )
is handled by a catch-up on an ordinary run: each one whose mirror is local is
hashed once. If the bytes match the signed sha256, the mirror's size and time
are
recorded and a stat vouches for it from then on; if not, the order above applies
as for any other mirror the index does not vouch for. An evicted mirror is never
read for this (the flags are read in batches, one
.BR stat (1)
per few hundred files). The catch-up is bounded so a first run over a large
archive does not stall: it may use 20 seconds of a run (see
.BR CCARCHIVE_LEGACY_BUDGET_MS ),
always checks at least one entry, and reports progress
.RI ( "N older index entries checked, M remain" ;
.I legacyChecked
and
.I legacyRemaining
under
.BR \-\-json ).
The bound is a latency limit for a one-off catch-up, not a size taken from any
archive: each entry is checked once ever, so any archive converges, and until
it does the unchecked entries are trusted as signed, as before, and
.B \-\-verify
still reads every one.
.PP
The append check (see
.BR INTEGRITY )
is only ever fed entries the index vouches for: an entry left lagging by a dead
run is repaired from the journal first, and an entry with no recorded length (a
legacy
.B fromArchive
one, say after a restore) takes its reference from the mirror's own checked
length before an append-only record is replaced \(em or, when the mirror is
evicted, the replacement waits and is reported
.BR "NOT ARCHIVED" .
A living document with no recorded length is kept as a version, which needs no
read.
.SH LOCK
One run at a time per archive. An ordinary run,
.B \-\-restore
and
.B \-\-rekey
take an exclusive lock for the destination; the read-only modes
.RB ( \-\-verify ,
.BR \-\-audit ,
.B \-\-lock\-status
and any
.BR \-\-dry\-run )
take none. The lock is a small file beside the signing key (see
.BR FILES ),
created whole in one step, recording the holder's pid, host, the start time of
that process (so a pid the system later hands to another process is not mistaken
for the holder), when the run started, and a heartbeat refreshed at every
checkpoint. A second run refuses, exit 2, naming the holder.
.PP
.B ccarchive \-\-lock\-status
tells
.BR free ,
.B held
by a live run since a given time, or
.B "STUCK: holder not running"
(exit 1). A lock is stuck when it was taken on this host and its process is no
longer running, or its pid now belongs to a process that started at another
time.
.B ccarchive \-\-clear\-lock
clears a stuck lock, and refuses while the holder is alive. A run that finds a
provably stuck lock clears it itself and says so on standard error. A lock
taken on another host cannot be judged from here: it is reported, never cleared
automatically, and
.B \-\-clear\-lock
refuses it; delete the file by hand only if you are sure that run is gone.
.SH AUDIT
.B \-\-verify
asks one question \(em is the
.I archive
still intact against its own manifest?
.B \-\-audit
asks the other \(em has the
.I live
store drifted from what was archived? It hashes every live transcript and
places it in one bucket:
.PP
.B synced
(live bytes match the recorded sha256; the healthy majority),
.B grown
(the archived bytes are a strict prefix of the live file \(em a plain append
since the last run, which the next
.B ccarchive
will capture),
.B mutated
(the live file diverges from the archived copy by something other than growth
\(em rewritten, or shrunk/truncated \(em and is an append-only record),
.B revised
(the same divergence for a living document, a memory note or subagent metadata:
normal, counted, not drift \(em the next run keeps the old version),
.B renamed
(a live file whose content matches an archived transcript recorded under a
different path that is now gone from the live store),
.B new
(an unarchived live file matching nothing archived) and
.B pruned
(an archived transcript with no live counterpart \(em the expected steady
state once Claude Code's cleanup has run).
.PP
Only
.B mutated
and
.B renamed
(and a live file the audit could not read) are drift: the archive no longer
equals the live session's history, or is
keyed by a stale path. Those exit non-zero and are listed by name;
.BR grown ,
.B new
and
.B pruned
are normal and only counted. The audit reads both trees and writes nothing.
.PP
The audit compares against the signed index, so it checks the manifest's
signature first, over the exact bytes it then parses, and reports the state (as
.B \-\-verify
does; under
.B \-\-json
it is the
.I signature
object). Results taken from an index whose signature is anything but
.B verified
are never presented as clean: the report says the index is not verified, never
prints the all-clear line, and exits 1.
.SH DATALESS
The default archive destination is iCloud Drive, and macOS's
.I Optimise Mac Storage
may evict the local bytes of an archived
.I .gz
it judges unused, leaving a
.B dataless
placeholder: the content is still in the cloud and faults back on the next read,
but no bytes sit on disk. Reading such a file re-downloads it. That is fine for
a deliberate
.BR \-\-restore ,
but a whole-archive
.B \-\-verify
that re-hashed every file would drag the entire history back onto disk \(em
costly, and it defeats the point of eviction.
.PP
So
.B ccarchive
detects eviction (the
.B SF_DATALESS
bit in the file's
.BR st_flags ,
read via
.BR stat (1)
\(em Node exposes no
.I st_flags
field of its own) and, by default,
.B \-\-verify
.I skips
an evicted file, reporting it
.B evicted
\(em a bucket distinct from
.B missing
and
.BR mismatch ,
and
.I not
a failure: the copy is intact in the cloud, merely not local, so it does not
exit non-zero.
.B \-\-audit
likewise leaves a changed live file whose archived copy is evicted
.I undetermined
rather than faulting the archive back to classify it. Pass
.B \-\-materialise
to read the evicted files anyway (re-downloading them).
.PP
A run never needs to read an evicted mirror's bytes. Mirrors are written by
creating a new file and renaming it over the old one (see
.BR "ATOMIC WRITES" ),
so replacing an evicted placeholder does not open it; an in-place rewrite is
what
used to fail on one with
.I Unknown system error \-11
(macOS's
.BR EDEADLK ,
returned when the system will not download an evicted file on demand). A run
reads a mirror only when the signed index does not vouch for it, or to repair
the index from the intent journal (see
.BR TRUST ).
An evicted one is never read there: it is reported as
.B not checked (offloaded),
and so is any read that fails with that error. Not checked is not a failure: it
exits
.BR 0 ,
unless a changed live copy is waiting behind it (then
.BR "NOT ARCHIVED" ,
exit 1).
.PP
Detection is metadata-only \(em
.BR stat (1)
does
.I not
fault an evicted file back, and neither does the mtime check an ordinary run
uses \(em so archiving new sessions and writing the manifest never trigger a
bulk re-download. Off macOS, or on a non-iCloud volume, the bit is simply absent
and
the whole mechanism cleanly no-ops (nothing is ever mis-reported as evicted).
Because a fixture can never be genuinely evicted, this behaviour is exercised in
the tests through a simulation seam; the underlying
.B SF_DATALESS
read was verified read-only against a real evicted iCloud file.
.SH ATOMIC WRITES
Every file the tool writes into the archive \(em each mirror, the manifest and
its
signature \(em is written to a uniquely named temporary file in the same
directory
(\fI.<name>.<pid>.<random>.tmp\fR), flushed to disk, given its final
modification
time, and then renamed over the target. The existing file is never opened for
writing. So a run killed mid-write leaves the previous good copy in place
instead
of a truncated one that looks fresh, and a symbolic link planted at a mirror
path
is replaced, not followed: nothing is ever written through a link to somewhere
outside the archive. A symbolic link found
.I inside
the archive, in a directory on the way to a mirror, is refused: that mirror is
named in the report as not written, and the run fails at the end.
.PP
A temporary file left behind by a run that died is ignored by every walk,
.B \-\-verify
and
.BR \-\-audit ,
and is removed by the next ordinary run. A temporary file whose process is still
running belongs to another run and is left alone. Restore writes live files the
same way.
.SH ONE BAD FILE
A single bad file never aborts a run. A source file that cannot be read, a
source
directory that cannot be listed, or a source that vanishes between the walk and
its read is skipped and named in the report (the
.I unreadable
list under
.BR \-\-json ),
the rest of the run completes and the manifest is saved, and the run then exits
non-zero so it is not missed. A mirror that will not decompress is named in the
same way by
.B \-\-verify
and
.BR \-\-restore ,
and processing continues; a run that meets one the index does not vouch for
sets it aside (see
.BR TRUST ).
Anything that does stop a run \(em an
unreadable or untrusted
manifest, a lock held by another run, an unknown option, a destination that
cannot be written \(em ends in one line on standard
error, or a structured
.I {"error": ...}
object on standard output under
.BR \-\-json ,
and exit status 2; there is no stack trace.
.PP
Read-only modes (\fB\-\-verify\fR, \fB\-\-audit\fR, \fB\-\-lock\-status\fR and
any
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR) never rename or overwrite anything. \fB\-\-restore\fR is
not one of them: it writes live files under the source tree (atomically, by
rename), and refuses before writing what it should not. If the manifest is not
valid
JSON, every mode stops with an error and leaves it where it is. Invalid JSON
never carries a valid signature, so an ordinary run refuses it too (see
.BR TRUST ).
Only after the operator's deliberate
.B \-\-rekey
does an ordinary run set an invalid manifest aside, as
.IR manifest.json.corrupt\-<UTC\ timestamp> ,
never over an earlier set-aside copy, and then start a new, signed
one. A manifest that
exists but cannot be read (an I/O error, or an evicted placeholder) is not
treated as invalid and is never set aside: the run stops and changes nothing.
.SH RESTORE
.B \-\-restore
is the inverse of archiving: it decompresses archived transcripts back into the
live store, gunzipping each
.IR <dest>/<rel> .jsonl.gz
to
.IR <source>/<rel> .jsonl.
It has two shapes. Plain
.B \-\-restore
targets every transcript in the manifest \(em the tool for rebuilding a live
store that was wiped or lost, or seeding a new machine from the archive.
.B \-\-restore \-\-delta
targets only the drift
.B \-\-audit
reports \(em the
.BR mutated ,
.B pruned
and
.B renamed
buckets \(em repairing in place rather than rewriting everything.
.PP
Safety is
.B content-first,
because the archive is a point-in-time copy and a live file can legitimately be
.I ahead
of it. Restore never overwrites an
.B identical
live file (nothing to do), and never overwrites a live file the archived copy is
a strict
.B prefix
of \(em the
.B grown
bucket, an in-flight session that has appended since the last archive run.
Overwriting that would drop the live tail the archive never held, so
.B grown is not a restore target
\(em enforced by comparing bytes, not by trusting a modification time.
.PP
A live file that has diverged some
.I other
way (rewritten or truncated) but carries a
.B newer
modification time than the archived copy may be a fresh in-flight rewrite.
Restore
.B refuses
it \(em loudly, with a non-zero exit \(em rather than overwriting silently;
.B \-\-force
is the deliberate override. Targets with no live file at the restore path (the
.B pruned
and
.B renamed
cases) cannot clobber anything and are always written.
.PP
The
.B renamed
bucket restores the
.I old,
archived path. The archive is keyed by the path the transcript had when it was
captured; a rename left that path empty in the live store and moved the content
to a new one. Restore re-materialises the archived (old) path and leaves the
live renamed copy at its new path untouched \(em the content is byte-identical,
so this adds a copy under the old name rather than moving anything, and never
destroys the live rename. A restored file is stamped with the archived copy's
modification
time, so a following
.B \-\-audit
reads it as
.B synced
and a repeated restore is idempotent.
.PP
Restore checks before it writes. It first authenticates the manifest exactly as
a run does (see
.BR TRUST ),
and refuses an index it cannot. The gunzipped bytes of each target are compared
with the sha256 the manifest recorded, and a copy that does not match (bit rot,
tampering) is
.B refused
and reported, not written; even
.B \-\-force
does not override that, because the bytes are provably not what was captured. An
entry with no recorded sha256 cannot be checked, so it is refused as
.B unverified
unless
.B \-\-force
is given, in which case it is restored and marked unverified in the report. A
mirror that will not decompress is reported by name and the restore carries on.
.B \-\-dry\-run
previews the plan and writes nothing. Restore writes only under the source tree;
a manifest key that would resolve outside it is refused.
.PP
Restore returns the
.B current
version of each file only. The kept copies under
.I _versions/
and
.I _anomalies/
are never restore targets (see
.BR "KEPT COPIES" ,
which says how to get an older state by hand), and
.B \-\-restore \-\-delta
leaves a living document alone when the live one has simply been edited since
the last archive run (the audit's
.B revised
bucket).
.PP
Unlike
.B \-\-verify
and
.BR \-\-audit ,
.B \-\-restore
does
.I not
skip an evicted
.RB ( dataless )
archive file: restoring means getting its content back, so faulting it down from
iCloud is exactly the intended read, not a gratuitous one. See
.BR DATALESS .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_DEST
The archive directory, when
.B \-\-dest
is not given. Default: a
.I cc-transcripts
folder in the operator's iCloud Drive.
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE
Where the signing key lives. Also relocates the
.I ccarchive\-state/
directory (intent journal and lock), which sits beside it. Default:
.IR ~/.claude/ccarchive\-signing.key .
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_HISTORY
Where the top-level prompt history is read from (and restored to). Default:
.I history.jsonl
one level above the source directory.
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_LEGACY_BUDGET_MS
The slice of one run, in milliseconds, the legacy-entry catch-up may use (see
.BR TRUST ).
Default 20000; 0 checks exactly one entry a run. Non-numeric values are ignored.
.PP
The rest are
.B test seams.
They exist so the tests can drive situations that cannot be produced on demand
(an eviction, a crash). They are read on every real run, so
.B do not set them
outside a test: the second kills every run.
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_SIMULATE_DATALESS
Comma-separated path fragments to treat as iCloud-evicted (dataless), so the
skip-and-report logic can be exercised on files that can never truly be evicted.
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_CHECKPOINT_EVERY
How many mirrors a run writes between manifest saves (a positive integer;
default 50). Lowered by tests so a checkpoint is reachable on a small tree. See
.BR SIGNING .
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_TEST_FAIL_AFTER
Make the run die after writing this many mirrors: by a thrown error (the catch
path saves the manifest), or with
.B CCARCHIVE_TEST_FAIL_HOW=kill
by SIGKILL the instant that mirror lands, before its index entry exists \(em the
hard-kill shape only the intent journal can repair.
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_SCHEDULER_LOG
A file path. When set, every
.B launchctl
or
.B systemctl
command the schedule options would run is appended to that file, one JSON array
of the arguments per line, and
.B not run.
Every test sets it, so no test can reach a real scheduler. With it set,
.B \-\-schedule\-status
says its loaded/enabled answers are pretended.
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_SCHEDULER_EXIT
With the seam above set: the exit status the pretended scheduler commands report
(default 0). Lets a test rehearse a scheduler that says no.
.TP
.B CCARCHIVE_TEST_FAIL_HOW
Set to
.B kill
to make
.B CCARCHIVE_TEST_FAIL_AFTER
a hard kill instead of a thrown error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/
The source: Claude Code's raw session logs and the sidecars and memory beside
them (read-only to this tool). See
.B CAPTURE
for exactly which files are taken.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/history.jsonl
The top-level prompt/command history, one level above the source root, also
captured (read-only). Relocate with
.BR CCARCHIVE_HISTORY .
.TP
.I <dest>/<rel>/<name>.gz
An archived file \(em the gzip of one captured source file (a transcript, a
subagent
.IR .meta.json ,
a tool-result sidecar or a memory
.IR .md ).
.TP
.I <dest>/_external/history.jsonl.gz
The archived top-level history, under a reserved
.I _external/
path that cannot collide with an encoded project directory.
.TP
.I <dest>/manifest.json
The integrity manifest: one sha256 (plus size and archive time, and the mirror's
own size and time) per archived file, of every captured class alike, and one
entry per kept copy.
.TP
.I <dest>/manifest.json.sig
The detached manifest signature (HMAC-SHA256 over the manifest bytes). See
.BR SIGNING .
.TP
.I <dest>/manifest.json.sig.next
A signature written ahead of its manifest, present only for an instant, or after
a run was killed in that instant. See
.BR SIGNING .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/ccarchive-signing.key
The signing key (hex, mode 0600), off the archive volume. Override with
.BR CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE .
Back it up out-of-band \(em a new machine needs it to verify.
.TP
.I <dest>/_untrusted/<UTC stamp>/<rel>.gz
A file a run found in the archive that the signed index does not vouch for and
that was not identical to the live copy: moved here, never deleted, never
adopted, and left out of every walk, verify and restore. See
.BR TRUST .
.TP
.I <dest>/_anomalies/<UTC stamp>/<rel>.gz
The changed live version of an append-only record that shrank or was rewritten
in
place; the archived mirror is untouched. Signed, never deleted, never walked as
a
mirror. See
.BR "KEPT COPIES" .
.TP
.I <dest>/_versions/<UTC stamp>/<rel>.gz
The previous mirror of a living document that changed other than by appending,
renamed here before the new one was written. Signed, never deleted, never walked
as a mirror. See
.BR "KEPT COPIES" .
.TP
.I <dest>/manifest.json.sig.orphan\-<UTC stamp>
A signature that was left with no manifest beside it, moved aside by
.BR \-\-rekey .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/ccarchive\-state/<id>.journal
The intent journal for one destination
.RI ( <id>
is a hash of the destination's real path): one signed record per mirror a run
is about to write. Mode 0600, in a 0700 directory beside the signing key (it
follows
.BR CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE ).
Present only while a run is going, after one died part way, or while a record
waits on an evicted mirror. See
.BR TRUST .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/ccarchive\-state/<id>.lock
The lock for one destination, while a run that writes holds it (or after one
died). See
.BR LOCK .
.TP
.I <dest>/.<name>.<pid>.<random>.tmp
A temporary file of a write in progress, or left by a run that died. See
.BR "ATOMIC WRITES" .
.TP
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.ccarchive.archive.plist
.TQ
.I ~/Library/Logs/ccarchive.log
The macOS schedule and its log. Present only after
.BR \-\-install\-schedule .
See
.BR SCHEDULING .
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/ccarchive.service
.TQ
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/ccarchive.timer
The Linux schedule. Present only after
.BR \-\-install\-schedule .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ccarchive
Mirror new or changed sessions into the archive.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-dry\-run
Preview what a run would archive without writing anything.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-verify
Confirm every archived transcript still matches its recorded sha256 and the
manifest's signature is intact \(em run this after restoring the archive to a
new machine (bring the signing key too, or the signature reads as unverifiable).
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-rekey
Roll the signing key and re-sign the manifest \(em after suspected key exposure,
or on a cadence.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-lock\-status
Is a run holding the archive, or did one leave a stuck lock?
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-audit
Report whether any live transcript has drifted (been rewritten, truncated or
moved) from what the archive preserved.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-restore \-\-delta \-\-dry\-run
Preview the transcripts a delta restore would replace, writing nothing.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-restore
Rebuild the live store from the archive \(em restore every archived transcript,
skipping any that already match or that the live store is ahead of.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-install\-schedule \-\-at 02:30
Archive every day at 02:30 local time from now on, until
.BR \-\-uninstall\-schedule .
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-dest ~/backup/cc
Archive somewhere other than iCloud Drive.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-json | jq '.sessions, .subagentTranscripts, .inArchive.sessions'
How many sessions are in the live store, how many subagent transcripts, and how
many sessions the archive holds (pruned ones included).
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-json | jq .versionsKept
Which living documents had their previous copy kept on this run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success (including a run or verify with nothing to do).
.B 1
means the command finished and found something that needs a person.
.B 2
means it could not finish: one clean line on standard error, or a structured
error object under
.BR \-\-json ,
and no stack trace.
.PP
An ordinary run exits
.B 1
\(em after completing everything else, except for the first two \(em when it:
.IP \(bu 2
refuses an archive destination inside a git work tree
.RB ( \-\-allow\-repo\-dest
overrides), before doing anything;
.IP \(bu 2
fires the layout-drift alarm (zero transcripts found while the manifest is
non-empty), before doing anything;
.IP \(bu 2
finds an
.B anomaly
\(em an append-only record that shrank or was rewritten in place, whether newly
kept aside or
.B STILL DIFFERS
from an earlier run
.RB ( \-\-force
resolves it);
.IP \(bu 2
skipped a source or a mirror it could not read, or refused to write a mirror
through a symbolic link inside the archive, or found a source under a reserved
area;
.IP \(bu 2
set a file aside under
.IR _untrusted/ ;
.IP \(bu 2
left a changed live copy
.B NOT ARCHIVED
because the copy it would replace could not be checked; or
.IP \(bu 2
found an intent journal record whose signature failed.
.PP
These exit
.BR 0 :
repairing the index from the journal, adopting a mirror identical to its live
copy, refreshing an entry's size and time, the legacy catch-up, keeping a
version
of a living document (reported, expected), and clearing a stuck lock. A
.B \-\-dry\-run
exits as the real run would, for what it can tell. See
.BR INTEGRITY ,
.BR "KEPT COPIES" ,
.BR TRUST ,
.B ATOMIC WRITES
and
.BR "ONE BAD FILE" .
.PP
.B \-\-verify
exits
.B 1
when the archive does not verify: any
.BR mismatch ,
.BR missing ,
.B corrupt
or
.B UNMANIFESTED
file (kept copies are checked too), or a manifest signature that is not
.B verified
\(em a
.BR MISMATCH ,
a different-key signature, an
.B unsigned
manifest, or an
.B unverifiable
one (no key). See
.BR SIGNING .
.B \-\-audit
exits
.B 1
when the live store has drifted: any
.B mutated
or
.B renamed
file, or a live file it could not read \(em or when the index's signature is not
.B verified
(see
.BR AUDIT ).
Growth, new files, edited living documents and pruning are expected and exit
.BR 0 .
An
.B evicted
file (dataless, skipped by
.B \-\-verify
or left undetermined by
.BR \-\-audit ,
or a mirror a run did not check) is
.I not
a failure and exits
.B 0
\(em its bytes are intact in the cloud. See
.BR DATALESS .
.B \-\-restore
exits
.B 1
when it has
.I nothing to restore
(no manifest, or an empty one), when it refuses to overwrite a live file newer
than the archived copy
.RB ( \-\-force
overrides), when it refuses an archived copy that fails its recorded sha256 or
has
none, or when a target cannot be restored (a manifest entry whose
.I .gz
is gone or will not decompress, or a file it could not read). A restore that
only
writes, skips identical or ahead files, or has nothing to do exits
.BR 0 .
.B \-\-lock\-status
exits
.B 1
when the lock is stuck or was taken on another host, else
.BR 0 .
.PP
.B 2
is also what an unknown or removed option, an unreadable or invalid manifest in
a read-only mode, a manifest a run or restore cannot authenticate (see
.BR TRUST ),
a lock held by another run (or a
.B \-\-clear\-lock
of a live or foreign one), a run that failed part way with an error (a full
disk, say), and any other I/O failure that stops a run (for example a
destination that cannot be written) exit with. A run killed outright has no exit
status to give: see
.BR SIGNING ,
and the journal under
.BR TRUST ,
for what it leaves and how the next run repairs it.
.SH NOTES
The default destination is the operator's iCloud Drive, derived at runtime
from the home directory; no personal path is baked into the tool. It reads the
source read-only and writes only under the destination, plus the signing key at
.I ~/.claude/ccarchive-signing.key
.RB ( CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE
to relocate) and the intent journal and lock in a
.I ccarchive\-state
directory beside it. Under
.BR \-\-restore ,
it writes live files under the source tree. A destination inside a git work tree
is refused, and the check resolves symbolic links, so a destination that is a
link into a work tree is refused too.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cctranscript (1),
.BR ccrepo (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR zgrep (1)
.PP
